The Other Side of the Leash

The last thing Orion remembered as a dog was the smell of rain on hot pavement and the tug of his red leash slipping from his owner's hand.

Then the world folded.

The alley behind the apartment complex stretched impossibly long, the air twisting like water in a shaken bowl. Light bent inward, spiraling into a shimmering oval that hummed with a sound Orion felt in his bones rather than his ears. Instinct screamed run, but curiosity—his strongest instinct of all—pulled him forward.

He leapt.

When Orion opened his eyes, the first thing he noticed was that he had eyes in a new way. Everything was sharper, flatter, more distant. The second thing he noticed was the pain—an unfamiliar ache in places he had never possessed before.

Arms. Legs. Fingers.

Orion screamed, but the sound that came out was a human voice.

He scrambled backward, staring at himself. Smooth skin. Five fingers on each hand. Long limbs tangled in unfamiliar clothing. His heart pounded as panic surged—he tried to wag his tail and felt nothing.

"No, no, no," he said aloud, stunned by the words forming so easily. "This isn't right."

The alley was gone. In its place stood a wide street paved with pale metal tiles that reflected a lavender sky. Towers curved upward like frozen waves, threaded with glowing lines that pulsed softly, as if the city itself had a heartbeat.

"You okay, man?"

Orion looked up to see a girl about his apparent age crouched a few feet away. She had copper-brown skin, short hair shaved on one side, and goggles resting on her forehead. Behind her hovered a small, cube-shaped machine that whirred gently.

"I—I don't know," Orion said. "I was a dog."

The girl blinked. Then she smiled.

"Yeah," she said. "Wormhole sickness will do that."

Before Orion could protest, two more people joined her.

The first was a tall, broad-shouldered man with silver tattoos running like circuitry down his arms. His voice was calm and steady. "Lila, he's destabilized. You can see it in his posture."

The third was… unusual. A floating figure made of light and shadow, vaguely human in shape but constantly shifting, as though it couldn't decide what it wanted to be.

"I am called Kesh," the being said, its voice echoing softly inside Orion's head. "And you, traveler, are very far from where you began."

Orion's knees buckled. Lila caught him before he fell.

"Okay," she said briskly. "First obstacle: gravity plus legs you don't know how to use. We'll start there."

Learning to walk as a human turned out to be humiliating.

Orion kept leaning forward too far, forgetting he didn't have four legs anymore. His balance was wrong. His senses were wrong. Worst of all, smells were dull—like trying to read a book through fog.

They brought him to a quiet plaza where soft light flowed from the ground instead of lamps. Lila showed him how to place one foot in front of the other. The tattooed man—who introduced himself as Jax—stood nearby, ready to catch him if he fell.

"This dimension reshapes visitors to fit local physics," Jax explained. "Your consciousness stayed intact, but your instincts didn't update."

"I just want to go home," Orion said.

Kesh drifted closer. "Home is a coordinate in space and self," it said. "You have lost both."

That was the second obstacle: identity.

Orion kept forgetting to use words instead of body language. He tried to bark warnings. He reached for doors with his mouth. When startled by a passing transport vehicle, he dropped instinctively to all fours, earning curious stares.

Lila handed him a reflective panel.

"Look," she said gently.

Orion stared at the human face looking back at him—dark hair falling into unfamiliar eyes that nonetheless held something deeply familiar. His eyes.

"I don't know who this is," he whispered.

"You're still you," Lila said. "Just… upgraded and extremely confused."

They took him to a food stall. Orion inhaled eagerly, only to recoil.

"Why does everything smell so… flat?"

Jax laughed. "Third obstacle," he said. "Human senses are weaker than canine ones. You'll adapt."

Orion wasn't sure he wanted to.

The final obstacle revealed itself at the edge of the city.

Beyond the towers lay a vast shimmering rift suspended in midair—an open wormhole, its surface rippling like liquid glass. Energy pylons anchored it in place, humming with power.

"That's the nearest stable gateway," Lila said. "But it won't open for just anyone."

Orion stared at it, heart pounding. "That's my way back?"

"Possibly," Kesh said. "But the wormhole responds to intention. It must recognize you as what you were."

"How?" Orion asked.

Jax knelt beside him. "You trusted curiosity more than fear when you came through the first time," he said. "You'll need that again."

As they approached the platform, alarms flared. The wormhole destabilized, ripples turning violent.

"Too much cognitive noise," Lila muttered. "He's thinking like a human now."

Orion closed his eyes.

He thought of running through grass. Of the sound of his owner's laugh. Of loyalty without words. Of joy that lived entirely in the present moment.

He stopped trying to understand.

The wormhole calmed.

Light reached outward, wrapping around him like a familiar leash.

"It's working!" Lila shouted.

Orion turned back to them.

"Thank you," he said, meaning it with every part of himself.

Kesh inclined its shifting head. "Whatever form you take," it said, "you are welcome in the universe."